{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman in the Latin Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. He served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. His life was filled with political intrigue and military conflict as he navigated the complex relationships between Christian states and Muslim powers. Raymond inherited his title at a young age after the murder of his father. He spent many years as a regent for the young Baldwin IV who later became the leper king. His role as regent placed him at the center of power struggles within the crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born around 1140 to Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was assassinated by the Hashashin sect in 1152. The young Raymond then assumed control over the County of Tripoli despite his tender years. He faced immediate threats from neighboring powers and internal rivals who sought to undermine his authority. His mother Hodierna played a significant role in securing his position during these early turbulent times. He eventually traveled to Antioch to marry Eschiva of Bures which strengthened his political alliances in the region.\n\n*Regency and Political Maneuvering*\nThe death of King Amalric in 1174 left the kingdom in a precarious state. Raymond became regent for the nine-year-old Baldwin IV. This position made him the most powerful man in the kingdom for several years. He clashed with other nobles such as Guy of Lusignan who sought greater influence. Raymond also faced accusations of treason when he allegedly conspired with Saladin. These accusations arose from his desire to prevent a disastrous war that he believed the kingdom could not win. His caution often put him at odds with more aggressive factions within the Christian leadership.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Death*\nRaymond fought in several major battles against the forces of Saladin. He commanded troops at the Battle of Montgisard where the Crusaders achieved a stunning victory. He later participated in the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187. This defeat led to the collapse of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the loss of most Christian territories. Raymond fell ill shortly after the battle and died in 1187 at his castle in Tripoli. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. He left behind a legacy of complex political maneuvering and attempts to preserve the Christian presence in the Holy Land through diplomacy as well as force. His story illustrates the challenges faced by the crusader states in their final decades. The lack of unity among the Christian leaders contributed significantly to their eventual downfall against the united Muslim forces led by Saladin.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0007059681}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.02656e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006733206}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.07996386}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.006285532}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.87977415}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.044665262}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.064134814}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.340536}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -4.3273907e-05}, {"token": " Latin", "logprob": -0.45960814}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -1.7913535}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00011265912}, {"token": " Jerusalem", "logprob": -5.3644326e-06}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.008293353}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -3.8147045e-06}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.00012935045}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.04634939}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.019534487}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.3755955}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.004736417}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0027064318}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.192093e-0